{"title":"Alpe d’Huez during the Tour de France: Cycling’s biggest party","link":"https://www.cyclist.co.uk/in-depth/alpe-d-huez-fans-cycling-tour-de-france","date":1794567243000,"content":"<p><a href=\"https://www.cyclist.co.uk/\">Cyclist</a><br />\n<a href=\"https://www.cyclist.co.uk/in-depth/alpe-d-huez-fans-cycling-tour-de-france\">Alpe d’Huez during the Tour de France: Cycling’s biggest party</a></p>\n\n<p>Alpe d’Huez is by no means the Tour de France’s toughest peak. The climb is only half the length of Mont Ventoux and its summit is some 800m lower than the Col du Galibier. Neither of those climbs, however, nor any other, can match the decibels produced on the Alpe.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Over the past few decades this mountain has garnered its own legend, attracting fans from around the globe to bear witness to some of the Tour’s most defining moments. It has since become a bucket list attraction for any pro cycling fan, leading it to be branded by the level-headed newspaper <em>L’Équipe</em> as a ‘climb in the style of Hollywood’.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>So when Tour de France organisers promised the 2026 race would have back-to-back stage finishes on Alpe d’Huez, fans instinctively scrambled for the nearest hotel rooms and campsite pitches to guarantee a double bill on the sport’s loudest mountain.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Move over Maracanã and Azteca Stadium – for one weekend this summer, Alpe d’Huez became the biggest sporting arena on the globe, with a reported crowd of half a million people there to catch a glimpse of Tadej Pogačar riding up the mythical hairpins.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Between the caravans, Europop and mankinis, <em>Cyclist </em>speaks to some of those who made the sporting pilgrimage.</p>\n\n\n\n<p><a href=\"https://www.cyclist.co.uk/in-depth/alpe-d-huez-fans-cycling-tour-de-france#h-colin\"><img src=\"/media/628d4b1017f9c307e63bdb9fc8b90c98d5e6cbed1b5213a3d06e5c1e28a5971f.webp\" alt=\"\" /><em><strong><mark>Article continues below…</mark></strong></em></a></p>\n\n\n\n<f