0ca683861ea7edd957627311985074de79b7b970ea48139ff.webp 465w, /media/0d27ef7b8bd8a7de8c6f2faedf525357e53bd081940f624e039ad477d0558e52.webp 620w, /media/cb31412ce5395765da541851fbf820a0eacb29cfcd664d9b95e80f5641a9ebb3.webp 350w, /media/0130ff84fa635ee6504f18bda56635af7ab4d731421c4fca2fe10db5aff655a1.webp 1536w\" /><figcaption>Joe McGorty</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n<div></div>\n\n\n\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" width=\"647\" height=\"970\" src=\"/media/f92a598dd68cb37d27e74facc19eb28c9183a4d6642b98cec67d71d215b817a2.webp\" alt=\"Alpe d'Huez fans\" srcset=\"/media/f92a598dd68cb37d27e74facc19eb28c9183a4d6642b98cec67d71d215b817a2.webp 647w, /media/4bf4a56f83237a9d2d8af4a3e694da7ed4bcd2409a9bb4562665a9cfb6f6ecce.webp 200w, /media/77199cd8cb75de24f0c2335ed2bfea3fd9803a361cff59160f7fec9b12126c8b.webp 768w, /media/fb6af73b0165a28fe19ad90c23d6bf535e7abad4c80c173fc4c1ea53f7f9d6b9.webp 1024w, /media/8ae3fd44dad10de988d41cf80d11e5977ca7b261eee3ceff1846aa1b9b9869da.webp 1366w, /media/a7a4040d0ec1a8b99bb3a907531864c809f6e852708ed9356931ef0f6d3be2d7.webp 750w, /media/9a1aaecd532004ae519870fd36c4435bde01fde5a8dfebafeed50a26acebc459.webp 1140w, /media/3a5b08fb81fae3a1466c8f873a66a8d9741463cc8e367e827855578414231cac.webp 1536w\" /><figcaption>Colin from Belgium has camped on Alpe d’Huez for five days to bag a good spot, and says it has been one long party.</figcaption><figcaption>Joe McGorty</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n<h3>Colin</h3>\n\n\n\n<p><em>from Belgium</em></p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘I’ve spent five nights sleeping inside a tent here. I’ve got neck pain, back pain, I’ve almost lost my voice, but it’s worth it. We’ve been having a party for the past five days, but today is the one we’ve been waiting for. People have been walking and cycling up here since 4am, so we had an early start to get the party started and make sure they didn’t miss out.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘We have three speakers, so if one runs out of charge or breaks, we’ve always got another on standby. My favourite thing to play is the Dutch song “Links Rechts”. Everyone here knows it and it’s great to see people jumping from side to side together.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘We were initially a group of 13, but several hundred have now come down to the corner. We’ve been chatting away and making new friends throughout the day. I remember some people from the last time I was here four years ago. That’s what the Tour’s about – making memories and connections that last a lifetime.’</p>\n\n\n\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" width=\"970\" height=\"647\" src=\"/media/84c6224c5067bbc600d7b260314470ec4f7238d1c183d3cb0c08016e795f0000.webp\" alt=\"Alpe d'Huez fans\" srcset=\"/media/2a58d800a2c2076702f599bed210e07b4550f87ad15d6d53592d65a7ceab6ad2.webp 970w, /media/bd6782a1bc9a03613cb92156b5abe44f73c6af1484ccd0d7d2052488f9f2bd40.webp 300w, /media/a49e4b65c92e3a0e34d351c4961e5dcedcf12ce3483998c30238e98381fb8de3.webp 768w, /media/b58f448831fe542de91a6fd9f2fde8dc4b34754b856648f8f20a3af296ac6f19.webp 750w, /media/e03eef3710498e689a80a254ade562447ec717b99da3157b5a614de765d158a5.webp 1140w, /media/968b6a0303801c51145ea3aa9c30c1ad661d1f723d29e137d3b77c4db7da631b.webp 465w, /media/d7c1ecd65e110667d3531f86b26cca5d731b47500542af489cd03da0d1f9e8bb.webp 620w, /media/2ed7a3a5a3380e77e45ca818c4af23f51b1fb2ce744f9afb8196f2fabad73825.webp 350w, /media/59adb5ca9ad5f5aea1d41d0762e6de7adf204eb39a08239a790a7e03b0c3de2a.webp 1536w\" /><figcaption>Belgians Thomas, Julien, Kevin and Jordan have attracted plenty of attention in their Quinn Simmons costumes – and are hoping for a high five from the man himself.</figcaption><figcaption>Joe McGorty</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n<h3>Thomas, Julien, Kevin and Jordan</h3>\n\n\n\n<p><em>from Belgium</em></p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘We’ve been in the Alps all week – around ten days already. Today we chose to dress up as Quinn Simmons because we like his character, his look, the energy, the aggression on the bike. He’s been active during every stage of the race so far.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘We haven’t been dressed like this for ten days though. This is the first day we’ve been in fancy dress. We bought the gear off AliExpress and we have some wigs and stick-on moustaches to wear once the race gets closer. He’ll probably be dropped by the time he reaches us, but getting a high five from Quinn would be great.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘We only got off the cable car about 30 minutes ago, but we’ve already had around 20 people ask for photos. We’re expecting a lot of extra attention – some Americans have already been quite loud.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘We’re not sure where the best hairpin is to watch the race today, but we’ll find a way to hunt down the best atmosphere. We’ve probably had about two beers each for breakfast. We’ve got two rucksacks filled with drinks, so I reckon we have about 40 to get us through the day. We’re Belgian – the beers are never far.’</p>\n\n\n\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" width=\"647\" height=\"970\" src=\"/media/fa42daa350990856aea7e5802ad7642a05b5084e5849e4cfb57e72029346551f.webp\" alt=\"Alpe d'Huez fans\" srcset=\"/media/fa42daa350990856aea7e5802ad7642a05b5084e5849e4cfb57e72029346551f.webp 647w, /media/716e14d699ca585f19d0456b82cc538d153fb08e2e71ad9832b5a1a5b88748a2.webp 200w, /media/4c2e5ac9ad2582060e6f18def6f200fde31a861a5dedc4d7c9d6b633b6e2cc3b.webp 768w, /media/30548c0303b24e5537018e7171070b8305ef3fbe3551f3c6161071f90c9da4bc.webp 1024w, /media/d4718e263272e36241e6e348a443a22568161532c4d610ad6e373582d1385aa8.webp 1366w, /media/acf859b13f9d8ccdeef164042d5415d7f3a7c612ee3e3fbca99aad0ead7bdf00.webp 750w, /media/fb0f1e7b837db6753d9e5199e19f134c69592e43b0234b538b7d8ff778043b32.webp 1140w, /media/8528d4fcaea14d99344873d2b63f3299927f6b84130d7a56e3462484d66d4b2b.webp 1536w\" /><figcaption>Jean-Louis is local and has noticed more French fans in the wake of Paul Seixas’s rise – he may even have to finish lunch early to bag a good vantage point.</figcaption><figcaption>Joe McGorty</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n<h3>Jean-Louis</h3>\n\n\n\n<p><em>from France</em></p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘We’ve lived in Huez since 2001 but my wife was born here. We’re not cyclists as such, but we’ve got lots of Tour memories. This year my cousin is here. We’ve never considered putting our house up on Airbnb or anything when the Tour comes. This is a moment for family and friends.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘Every three or four years this town becomes something else. Every time, the Tour feels more popular. People flock to the bends nowadays, but I think we used to be able to get closer to the riders.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘There are more caravans too. They’ve taken all the parking spaces in the town. Some have been parked outside our house for a week. I think there are more people this year because of Paul Seixas. I’ve noticed more French people in particular. He is our golden boy and when we have a Frenchman doing well, it sparks national euphoria.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘We’ll have our lunch on the balcony, then head down. We might have to go earlier than that to get the best spot.’</p>\n\n\n\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" width=\"886\" height=\"970\" src=\"/media/087ef33c69cfb617bd74a95598d6bbf8615b328eb3b9a49e6e0becf82e1f73d4.webp\" alt=\"Alpe d'Huez fans\" srcset=\"/media/087ef33c69cfb617bd74a95598d6bbf8615b328eb3b9a49e6e0becf82e1f73d4.webp 886w, /media/ea058e03f72b2d2c0bd439d18b86608862ac620fd230e2bbfcc92d27d15dc2c1.webp 274w, /media/f2dcb5c14b1c87a051cb5a27263e15346fd16f13df792ab2a3640ef48e521078.webp 768w, /media/0c38520f7ebe0a72dfd3b1b3fb3215b4ed68c1c03593b577d2dbce0fd2e4ad4b.webp 1404w, /media/88c28c8fe7437ac6db485de9532b72321dad76ae5156f820011e3cdeef2ce3bb.webp 750w, /media/a82f33282e1324c0f2d982b04bbb236fe254e387fa2b9a7236fc06f6c038570c.webp 1140w, /media/e6664467d464d4bc8302aa666fffb1d341d48472c0874bcd174a428e87b1878d.webp 1536w\" /><figcaption>Monica and Sofia (above) and Victor (below) have come all the way from Mexico to cheer on Isaac del Toro.</figcaption><figcaption>Joe McGorty</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" width=\"647\" height=\"970\" src=\"/media/c86a737d612b97112efb1b4bdc636df189abf0e0a7a5ba0375b0c516a1aff752.webp\" alt=\"Alpe d'Huez fans\" srcset=\"/media/c86a737d612b97112efb1b4bdc636df189abf0e0a7a5ba0375b0c516a1aff752.webp 647w, /media/8e3878af0188d49f8b457ffc57ea982cf00cd8701358eb021f20cf203553dd55.webp 200w, /media/5c25bdf21bd18bf15d48d4a7443c709d55f9c5062d4712bbbe0e19384b6ab41f.webp 768w, /media/dc856ed5cfb20faeee259c39ce9d9bf754231c52144d5f350b46c0d31d151bf3.webp 1024w, /media/ea222b4707ea04c2ad55b8725a4e87b0d6c99b5e7bebc86be49b36e2109bdbba.webp 1366w, /media/27b53794957d5ede6fc540dce67f9db6ff9e59e9d139a69a86aef12c5640fb7c.webp 750w, /media/50f401eb10d43d71b99d3e944de7245df649693a4a79329718da4b37d0f122fc.webp 1140w, /media/5ab72def1bf852f7be2f8094d56c2a8a95ceb09b7d9dcbf452a1d3f64a3074a6.webp 1536w\" /><figcaption>Joe McGorty</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n<h3>Victor, Monica, Sofia and Claudia</h3>\n\n\n\n<p><em>from Mexico</em></p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘We’re from Torito’s [Isaac del Toro’s] hometown. We’re almost family. She [Claudia] was his neighbour.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘This is our fourth or fifth Tour de France. We’ve been to Strade Bianche, the Giro, Tirreno-Adriatico – there were maybe six, eight or ten Mexicans at those races. Today there are probably a couple hundred around this one corner.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘A lot of our Mexican friends have been here for two or three days already. There’s definitely some tequila in the cool boxes around here somewhere, so if Torito wins we’ll get drunk together.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘Isaac’s having a massive impact back home. Finishing second at the Giro created a big boom for the cycling community, but now a culture is growing around the Tour de France. Most people didn’t really know what it was about. They knew it was a race in France but nothing else. People are talking about it this time. There’s even a channel dedicated to showing maybe five or six hours of the Tour. Before, you would see just a 30-minute highlight package.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘Even though we got knocked out of the World Cup, we are still happy because of Isaac. We’re not jealous of Tadej either. We want him to help Isaac win a Tour. Isaac will get his time.’</p>\n\n\n\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" width=\"970\" height=\"647\" src=\"/media/9a2515fa24a811eb71211c1bc95dc55cba62d274e76dbea16379b735d063e6ab.webp\" alt=\"Alpe d'Huez fans\" srcset=\"/media/20c433a7cc79511de8156c10e5a3f01f23116bd531d359670ef3cf5aad226adf.webp 970w, /media/0f32909b9ffb18b999564dfb4e0e3b0dea7ce5e3b77c2c72061d64f1493d3e03.webp 300w, /media/7dfdb288b18c26b57aeea6171ccb3719c5f6a5a148bfa65efa71e73b43bac6d7.webp 768w, /media/43b2b83d0cc10bb1c2a23dd3bb012b193a6d5c703bab79951abea9e027a07a42.webp 750w, /media/35d84a9131552afe4df2fbff2502b8f927eb6fc9e2797479e58ff23b433e8516.webp 1140w, /media/fcdd49e428e8201beda75fa0854a36af3dad82b7ae41b025c7902dd2c530441a.webp 465w, /media/e45c496fca60cbe1384e0c69f370125cd6d068e3351de6f464cee3121388cbe2.webp 620w, /media/44bab23a842c928772d33b89e550d8a068065aeadfa5497c88bfd81d70bbcad7.webp 350w, /media/f4d895f7582b4d747f012a5f0ed7535734ed7f8641cf126ebdec23e222d33b34.webp 1536w\" /><figcaption>Karel, José and Lars have travelled from the Netherlands and are determined to have a good time, but say Dutch corner has become overcrowded and is prone to drink-fuelled arguments.</figcaption><figcaption>Joe McGorty</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n<h3>José, Karel and Lars</h3>\n\n\n\n<p><em>from the Netherlands</em></p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘When the Tour route was announced, we knew we had to come here. It’s 1,200km from home but we drove it in one go. If you want a spot on Dutch Corner you have to arrive a week before.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘A Dutch priest would ring the church bell at this corner every time a Dutch rider won on Alpe d’Huez. That’s why it’s called Dutch Corner. We came here because of that. It’s like a big orange family. It’s often the same people here year after year. We don’t know each other in Holland but when we come here we all look out for each other. That atmosphere and the music, it’s what we call <em>gezelligheid</em>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘More Dutch people were here last time we came [in 2022]. There was more atmosphere in the days before, but this year it was a bit quiet. There were maybe ten Dutch people here during the week. In 2022 there were 20 or 30.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘Because it’s a good atmosphere people know to come here, so it often gets crowded. People get drunk and arguments start. There were some issues with the police before. It’s getting worse – and less Dutch – every year. It’s a shame.’</p>\n\n\n\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" width=\"647\" height=\"970\" src=\"/media/8de2381b6979de341693d9b0b51dd733873f10b34989c9c982166850a0a18415.webp\" alt=\"Alpe d'Huez fans\" srcset=\"/media/8de2381b6979de341693d9b0b51dd733873f10b34989c9c982166850a0a18415.webp 647w, /media/1b0c0b6e6b61ac17d31574190b930adbf8f412ba94539ba3cc832397464d1adc.webp 200w, /media/0aa8ba697963bd15095a9d7fd9434bb9472bf454e1749e0bc13f8ff636b91014.webp 768w, /media/7b99d82426e9b77f898f5d162c7ffc497bb14318886c73b4760c3129e152a9ea.webp 1024w, /media/081f284e77172d26be0626e7f9b3b1ef29fdf30096f81ec56f236de04ec426d6.webp 1366w, /media/925062cdcf6ff1022af0081149bb4d91d5507b1d511e85ccc87fcd617409c607.webp 750w, /media/ecee0903b145aab554e9004ce6158177b63cffb54a189294da45a5de9778b0ec.webp 1140w, /media/3d4b3a5af3804266bc1d33f5facc14f1169c54c94d932e8b77d8315d2090e8bd.webp 1536w\" /><figcaption>Meanwhile Ulf is part of what he hopes will become a new tradition on Alpe d’Huez: Danish corner.</figcaption><figcaption>Joe McGorty</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n<h3>Ulf</h3>\n\n\n\n<p><em>from Denmark</em></p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘We had a Danish Corner on the Col de la Loze last year. It was a big success so we felt we needed somewhere for the Danes to gather at this year’s race.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘Some Danes started following the Tour in Barcelona. It began with just a small group of friends but word spread about Danish Corner through social media. It has moved around France. Some have been following the race since the start, but a lot of the group have been together since Stage 15 [when the race reached the Alps].</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘You can always see a Danish flag when the Tour’s on TV. I think it’s been more noticeable this year. We’ve brought a piece of Denmark to France and I think we’ve challenged Dutch Corner today. Everyone here is wearing red – football shirts, flags, it’s everywhere. One guy has even painted his moustache red. We’ve got our own Danish beers, food and Faxe Kondi – a drink we call Danish gasoline.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘There’s great camaraderie here with hundreds of Danes looking out for each other. I have a little child at home, but when you have such good friends around, it helps with the homesickness.’</p>\n\n\n\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" width=\"647\" height=\"970\" src=\"/media/57e7b2a4df5be76ba6e6762aa78d79b2e9d47c63a9e71bf9cb8086718878dff6.webp\" alt=\"Alpe d'Huez fans\" srcset=\"/media/57e7b2a4df5be76ba6e6762aa78d79b2e9d47c63a9e71bf9cb8086718878dff6.webp 647w, /media/5ee90f2573dca2e74fe2ec717264440139ea46e4d3121229118cf3ac05499150.webp 200w, /media/bdb77ad19b35398bb188260bc64a7cefc5285e3bfb299cbae2f42499d75a932d.webp 768w, /media/a219794f0ee7e061cc545c9dfa492f72e4078d94ccb7cd7dedd1e1913310f370.webp 1024w, /media/f391e7eda590b6f2ab41d3c2e0493c8075c08bb90efc346128081689f46204ab.webp 1366w, /media/b273cc76e449880277cdb6072a19340d773e4845d2d12abe2f47110a20659c79.webp 750w, /media/98088c23ecb4c8d70450a3fb4f6a917cac5b0918285cf9ac634977dd0d13d325.webp 1140w, /media/ac4b2fcdc6264c0d24535826fdbcc6ae0deddcdd63425d9f8059f1bcb51f39bb.webp 1536w\" /><figcaption>Nataša has come from Slovenia to cheer on Tadej Pogačar, but is also sporting a sombrero for his teammate Isaac del Toro.</figcaption><figcaption>Joe McGorty</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n<h3>Nataša </h3>\n\n\n\n<p><em>from Slovenia</em></p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘I’ve been to the Giro and the basketball and football European Championships, but never the Tour de France. It’s my first time. I’ve been watching the Tour on TV since Armstrong, Pantani and Ullrich and I’d like to think that I would probably be here even if Pogačar wasn’t, but this year I have time, and I don’t want to miss my chance to see Pogi.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘I live near his home town but I’ve never seen him race in real life before. I heard that he’s underlined this stage, so it would mean everything to see him win.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘Every time I see Slovenian sport I wear this flag necklace. It’s from cheering at the basketball European Championship and the football Euros. I’m also wearing this hat [a sombrero] because Del Toro and Pogačar are such good friends.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘Slovenia is such a small country, so it’s a huge opportunity for our sportsmen to put us on the map. Right now, Pogačar’s our number one. Cycling was popular before him but now even more. The clubs don’t have space for new kids. Hopefully our government will pay to host the Tour de France soon.’</p>\n\n\n\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" width=\"647\" height=\"970\" src=\"/media/77076c0dd8f947073364617195d1ab97e1e603259baf31a3dc4e5e4a3b03bc46.webp\" alt=\"Alpe d'Huez fans\" srcset=\"/media/77076c0dd8f947073364617195d1ab97e1e603259baf31a3dc4e5e4a3b03bc46.webp 647w, /media/99b5e14873f524a37040e17da4d5c2c8b3d45172448539938793da76e2aa5939.webp 200w, /media/9120928003d28c23881386697a5bd8c643a95f37e5045f8a24826ce086cc16ee.webp 768w, /media/2caf3d4fe7a1b51cf2864604c841adba1c8ee10923f8ace88be68f810e67a078.webp 1024w, /media/b589f207987066c360609175845a82663f366dbe51de5629a42086128a5e4e28.webp 1366w, /media/ff6664a2c049c1dc8eadd746cc6cf38af75ae75b9ea8747ca14bc8b0a9aa6087.webp 750w, /media/89f3c491467b9cc2df2c1a2442034f4c8e382701653a7cf0a62658cfa7d058ef.webp 1140w, /media/a3e1896abf39b38a014e1087d5212f6ec15cde358d19121d365f3855a7ac39a6.webp 1536w\" /><figcaption>Didi the Devil made it to Alpe d’Huez despite having his van broken into in Barcelona – thankfully the thieves didn’t steal his costume.</figcaption><figcaption>Joe McGorty</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n<h3>Dieter ‘Didi the Devil’ </h3>\n\n\n\n<p><em>from Germany </em></p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘I’ve been following the Tour in this costume for 33 years. I’m 74 now. I’ve had a couple of health issues but I’m still jumping. It’s like an addiction.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘Fans and riders recognise me. People ask for photos and shout “El Diablo” at me. I’m not sure whether I’m a celebrity but I enjoy it. It’s my work.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘Someone asked how much I would have if I had a euro for every photo. The truth is, I don’t make any money from that. I have sponsorships, but I’m doing it for fun really. It’s a funny life. I bring a van with me for the race. It’s got a mattress inside. I was staying in a rough area in Barcelona, however, and my van was broken into before the first stage. They smashed one of the windows and stole some of my things. They didn’t take my devil costume, but I had to miss the race.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘I was here on the Alpe for the Étape du Tour last week. No other climb in this sport is like Alpe d’Huez. This is what cycling is all about.’</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.cyclist.co.uk/in-depth/alpe-d-huez-fans-cycling-tour-de-france\">Alpe d’Huez during the Tour de France: Cycling’s biggest party</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.cyclist.co.uk/\">Cyclist</a>.</p>","author":"Ewan Wilson","comments":"https://www.cyclist.co.uk/in-depth/alpe-d-huez-fans-cycling-tour-de-france#respond","siteTitle":"Cyclist.co.uk","siteHash":"561486e7070fa5351de9318ad0fa26c2a39332acba5fc7c7d1694a01d53d431e","entryHash":"6a1b16db523063333f59149f6be7129e067de603c38177265bd9fa96ee6b8367","category":"Cycling"}